response:
According to Section 3.1 of the Corporate Data Protection and GDPR Compliance Policy, processing of personal data must rely on at least one of the following lawful bases under Article 6 GDPR:

(a) Consent of the data subject;
(b) Necessity for the performance of a contract;
(c) Compliance with a legal obligation;
(d) Protection of vital interests;
(e) Performance of a task carried out in the public interest;
(f) Legitimate interests pursued by the Company, provided such interests are not overridden by the data subject's rights.

According to the internal corporate database context, a Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA) is mandatory prior to processing that is likely to result in high risk, as specified in Section 8.1 of the 'Corporate Data Protection and GDPR Compliance Policy' (Page 2).

The high-risk processing scenarios include:

(a) Systematic and extensive profiling with legal or similarly significant effects;
(b) Large-scale processing of Special Category Data;
(c) Systematic monitoring of publicly accessible areas on a large scale;
(d) Deployment of new technologies with novel or high-risk processing characteristics.

Therefore, a DPIA is mandatory in these high-risk scenarios.
